Article 10

1. Forms of violence against women and children include:a. Physical violence;
b. Sexual violence;
c. Economic violence;
d. Psychological violence.
2. The form of physical violence as referred to in paragraph (1) letter a is any act that results in pain, injury, wounds or disability to a person's body, miscarriage, fainting and or causing death.
3. The form of sexual violence as referred to in paragraph (1) letter b, is in the form of sexual harassment, forced sexual intercourse, forced sexual intercourse in an unnatural or unwanted manner, forced sexual intercourse with others for commercial purposes and or certain purposes. (4) The form of economic violence as referred to in paragraph (1) letter c is in the form of neglect of family members in the form of not providing a proper livelihood, care or maintenance.

(5) The form of psychological violence as referred to in paragraph (1) letter d is in the form of acts that result in fear, loss of self-confidence, loss of ability to act, feelings of helplessness and or severe psychological suffering to a person:

CHAPTER IV

OBLIGATIONS AND RESPONSIBILITIES

Article 6

1. The Regional Government is obliged and responsible to carry out efforts to prevent violence against women and children, in the form of: a. collecting data and information about victims of violence and laws and regulations;
b. conducting education on anti-violence values against women and children;

c. socializing laws and regulations related to the implementation of protection for victims of violence;
d. conducting monitoring and evaluation of the implementation of protection for victims of violence;
e. preparing reports on the implementation of protection for victims of violence.
2. To anticipate the occurrence of acts of violence against women and children, the Regional Government is obliged to provide and organize services for victims in the form of:a. establishing and facilitating the implementation of integrated service institutions for victims by involving community elements;
b. encouraging public awareness of the importance of protection for victims.
3. The Regional Government in carrying out its obligations as referred to in paragraph (1) and (2) must pay attention to the rights and obligations of parents, guardians, husbands or other persons who are legally responsible for the victim.

CHAPTER II

PRINCIPLES AND OBJECTIVES

Article 2

The principles of implementing protection for women and children victims of violence are:
a. Respect for the rights of victims;
b. Justice and gender equality;
c. Non-discrimination;
d. Best interests of victims.

BY THE GRACE OF GOD ALMIGHTY

THE REGENT OF KARANGANYAR, Considering:
a. b.
c. that every member of society has the right to obtain a sense of security and freedom from all forms of violence in accordance with the Pancasila philosophy and the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;

that all forms of violence constitute violations of human rights and crimes against human dignity as well as forms of discrimination that must be eliminated;

that victims of violence, the majority of whom are women and children, must receive protection from the government and/or society in order to avoid and be free from violence or threats of violence, torture, or treatment that degrades human dignity and worth;

that the number of cases of violence against women and children in Karanganyar Regency still occurs, while the regulation of the implementation of protection for women and children victims of violence has not been optimal;
e. that based on the considerations as referred to in letters a, b, c, and d, it is deemed necessary to regulate the implementation of protection for women and children victims of violence through a Karanganyar Regency Regulation;

In View of 1. 2.

